---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, howUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay the time-travel principle maps to (docs/domain.md, "The principle map"; Q12 ·
// Q-clock). The principle has no lab page yet, so the bakery explains it itself. Static, no
// island. Every claim about Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation stated it on the
// date in READ, and every number from the code names its file.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS['time-travel'];
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/time-clocks.pdf',
    title: 'Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events in a Distributed System',
    by: 'Communications of the ACM 21(7), July 1978, a copy hosted by its author',
    for: 'the happened-before relation, and logical clocks as counters that need no relation to physical time',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/',
    title: 'Durable Objects alarms',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'one alarm at a time per object, set in milliseconds since the Unix epoch, with a new setAlarm() replacing the old one; at-least-once execution, retried with exponential backoff from 2 seconds, up to 6 retries',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/cron-triggers/',
    title: 'Cron Triggers',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Workers documentation',
    for: 'a cron trigger runs on UTC',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks',
    title: 'Receive Stripe events in your webhook endpoint',
    by: 'Stripe’s documentation',
    for: 'a signed timestamp on every webhook, checked against a tolerance of five minutes by default in Stripe’s libraries',
  },
  {
    href: SITE_LAB,
    title: 'The lab',
    by: 'the lab index on ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'five lab pages, the Route, Queue, Cache, Backfill and Raft Labs, and none on clocks or time travel',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery lets a visitor move a sandbox’s business clock forward: wall time plus an offset, timers re-armed in business time, what stays on the wall clock, and the 14-day cap."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Most of what a bakery does is about later. This is how a visitor moves later closer: one
      offset the bakery owns, timers that know which clock they belong to, and the things that must
      never take the bakery’s time.
    </p>

    <h2>A week you cannot wait for</h2>
    <p>
      A visitor reaches the checkout and stops. In 15 minutes of bakery time the hold on their
      pickup place ends. A catering inquiry from last week is due a follow-up on the third open day
      after its quote. A campaign is scheduled for tomorrow morning. Each of these is real code with
      a timer, and none of it can be seen in a visit of ten minutes. The console therefore has a
      Clock with four buttons: 15 minutes, 1 hour, 1 day and Next opening, which goes to the next
      7:00 on a day the bakery opens.
    </p>

    <h2>What simpler designs do, and where they break</h2>
    <p>
      One design fires the timers by hand: a debug button that runs “expire this order now”. It
      shows the result and skips the cause. The order’s events would say it expired at a time when
      its hold had not ended, and every kind of timer would need a button of its own. Another moves
      the whole Worker’s clock. That moves every sandbox at once, and it moves things that are not
      the bakery’s to move: a cookie’s expiry, Stripe’s signature check, the nightly wipe. No lab
      page shows this one yet. The{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        lab’s index
      </a>{' '}
      is where the lab explains the family of techniques around it; as read on {READ} it has five
      labs and none on clocks.
    </p>

    <h2>Two clocks, and who owns each</h2>
    <p>
      The bakery keeps two clocks and never confuses them. Wall time is the platform’s. Business
      time is wall time plus the sandbox’s offset, whole seconds the Sandbox object keeps as{' '}
      <code>clock_offset_s</code>. One adapter reads the platform’s clock (in
      <code>workers/api/src/clock.rs</code>), and its two functions are the whole conversion:{' '}
      <code>business_at</code> adds the offset to a wall time, and <code>wall_at</code> takes it
      away. Business time is shown at America/Denver’s offset for that instant, so a sandbox born in
      summer reads <code>-06:00</code> and its seeded history agrees (<code>docs/roadmap.md</code>,
      PR 6).
    </p>
    <p>
      The name is borrowed. The logical clocks of the 1978 paper “Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of
      Events in a Distributed System” are counters with no tie to physical time: they exist to order
      events. The bakery’s business time is physical time shifted. The Lamport-like part is beside
      it: a counter, <code>clock_v</code>, that the Sandbox adds one to on every move, so that any
      object can tell a newer offset from an older one.
    </p>

    <h2>Later is the bakery’s question</h2>
    <p>
      The platform’s alarm knows only wall time: Cloudflare’s <code>setAlarm()</code>, as read on{' '}
      {READ}, takes milliseconds since the Unix epoch, and an object has one alarm at a time. So
      every object keeps a <code>timers</code> table, and a timer that belongs to the business
      carries two times: <code>due_business</code>, when the bakery wants it, and{' '}
      <code>due_wall</code>, when the platform should wake the object to ask (in
      <code>workers/api/src/objects/stream.rs</code>). The alarm is armed for the earliest{' '}
      <code>due_wall</code>. When it fires, the object reads business time and decides what is due.
      The alarm only wakes the object. Whether a hold has ended is decided by the aggregate, by
      business time.
    </p>
    <p>
      A click on 1 day sends the Sandbox an <code>AdvanceClock</code>. In one transaction it grows
      the offset by 86,400 seconds, adds one to <code>clock_v</code>, appends{' '}
      <code>ClockAdvanced</code> and stores the answer under the request’s key. Then it calls{' '}
      <code>/_clock</code> on every registered child whose <code>clock_v</code> is behind (in
      <code>workers/api/src/objects/child.rs</code>, <code>ClockUpdate</code>). Each child writes
      the new offset and recomputes <code>due_wall</code> from <code>due_business</code> for every
      timer that has one, then arms its alarm for the earliest. A day’s advance makes every unpaid
      hold’s wall time the past, so the alarm fires at once and the holds end in business-time
      order. A child that cannot be reached keeps its old <code>clock_v</code> in the Sandbox’s
      registry; the Sandbox logs <code>clock_children_missed</code> and its own <code>clock</code>
      timer tries again 30 seconds later. An update no newer than the one a child holds changes
      nothing, so the retries are safe.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three steps are lengths of business time. A day is 86,400 seconds, so across a change to or
      from daylight time the local hour shifts by one. Next opening is the exception, worked out by
      the calendar (<code>crates/domain/src/clock.rs</code>), so from a Sunday evening it lands on
      Tuesday at 7:00, Monday being closed.
    </p>

    <h2>What keeps the wall clock, and why</h2>
    <p>
      Anything that answers to someone outside the sandbox stays on wall time. The wipe at 10:00 UTC
      is a cron trigger, and cron triggers run on UTC (Cloudflare, read on {READ}); it is a promise
      on the privacy page and cannot be a visitor’s to move. A cookie and a Turnstile token expire
      by the browser’s and Cloudflare’s clocks. Stripe stamps its webhooks with its own time, and
      the bakery checks the signature within 300 seconds of wall time (<code>TOLERANCE_S</code> in{' '}
      <code>crates/app/src/stripe/webhook.rs</code>); in business time, a webhook after any advance
      would look a day old and be refused. The Analytics Engine points count the real account’s use.
      The loop guard’s 100 re-arms an object a day, and <code>Control</code>’s 30 minutes of
      simulated traffic a day, count UTC days, so skipping ahead never buys a fresh budget. The
      36-hour backstop and every event’s <code>recorded_at</code> are wall time as well.
    </p>

    <h2>A click that arrives twice</h2>
    <p>
      Each click is one intent with its own Idempotency-Key, and the buttons are disabled while one
      is in flight (<code>apps/web/src/lib/clock.ts</code>, <code>ClockClicks</code>). If the answer
      is lost, the next click on the same step sends the same key and the Sandbox replays the first
      answer, so the clock moves once. Two clicks that were both answered are two moves, which is
      what the visitor asked for. The key is for a request repeated by the network, not for a
      visitor who presses twice on purpose.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A cap of 14 days.</strong> The offset only grows, and never past 1,209,600 seconds
        (set as
        <code>MAX_OFFSET_S</code> in <code>crates/domain/src/clock.rs</code>). There is no way back
        until the wipe gives the visitor a new sandbox the next day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A quote you cannot watch expire.</strong> A quote lasts 14 open days, 16 or more
        calendar days, so no advance within the cap expires one made since birth;{' '}
        <code>docs/roadmap.md</code> (PR 7) leaves the choice open, a higher cap or a shorter
        validity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A call to every child on every move.</strong> A seeded sandbox has 36 children (from
        <code>docs/cost.md</code>, as built on 2026-11-06), so one click is at least 36 requests to
        other objects, plus a row written for each timer that moves.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two clocks to keep apart.</strong> Every timer is written with the clock it belongs
        to, and a mistake is silent: a business deadline set on wall time never moves.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in, order from <a href="/menu/">the menu</a> and stop at the checkout. In{' '}
      <a href="/app/">the console</a>, press 15 minutes under Clock and read the capacity editor’s
      line for that slot: one place fewer is taken. Press 1 day and read the activity feed for{' '}
      <code>ClockAdvanced</code>, then keep pressing until the clock refuses with its limit of 14
      days.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('time-travel')}>the time-travel principle on How it’s built</a>, to
      the decisions that chose it,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0004')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0004
      </a>{' '}
      and{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0005')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0005
      </a>
      , or to{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
